Hi Zhuxu,

Thanks for the v2, the overall direction (sharing one RTC devicetree node
between the RTC and sysreset drivers via regmap instead of hardcoding
physical addresses in the sysreset driver) is a nice cleanup. A few
comments below, mostly about error handling completeness and the
bind/probe relationship between patches 1 and 3.

No Reviewed-by/Acked-by tags have been picked up yet on this v2, so
please keep that in mind for v3.

=== Patch 1/3: rtc: cv1800b: Add seconds counter support ===

> +	priv->regmap = devm_regmap_init(dev, NULL, NULL, NULL);
> +	if (IS_ERR(priv->regmap))
> +		return PTR_ERR(priv->regmap);

This calls devm_regmap_init() with a NULL regmap_config, which means the
regmap width/stride come from the driver-model defaults and the full
"reg" range of the devicetree node. That's fine as long as the DT node
covers every offset this driver (and the sysreset driver bound onto the
same node) touches, up to MACRO_RG_SET_T (0x1498) here and
RTC_EN_WARM_RST_REQ (0x10cc) in patch 3. Please double check the actual
cv1800b RTC node's "reg" size is large enough for both consumers -- it's
not visible in this series since no devicetree patch is included.

> +static int cv1800b_rtc_bind(struct udevice *dev)
> +{
> +	if (!CONFIG_IS_ENABLED(SYSRESET_CV1800B))
> +		return 0;
> +
> +	return device_bind_driver_to_node(dev, "cv1800b_sysreset",
> +					  "sysreset", dev_ofnode(dev), NULL);
> +}

Binding the sysreset device onto the *same* ofnode as the RTC device
(dev_ofnode(dev), not a child node) is a deliberate trick to get a
second udevice with its own regmap pointed at the same register block.
This works in U-Boot, but it's worth spelling out in the commit message
since it's non-obvious, and it means two independent regmap instances
end up mapping the same physical range -- there's no shared regmap
or locking between the RTC and sysreset accesses. That's probably fine
given actual usage patterns, but please confirm there's no possibility
of concurrent access (e.g. RTC read racing a reset request) that would
want a single shared regmap instead of two independently-probed ones.

> +	ret = clk_get_by_name(dev, "rtc", &priv->clk);
> +	if (ret)
> +		return ret;
> +
> +	return clk_enable(&priv->clk);

Nit: if clk_enable() fails there's no clk_free()/clk_disable() cleanup
path, but since this is a bool Kconfig driver that's unlikely to be a
real issue in practice -- just flagging for completeness since the rest
of the driver is careful about regmap error propagation.

> +	ret = regmap_read(priv->regmap, SEC_PULSE_GEN, &value);
> +	if (ret)
> +		return ret;
> +	if (value & SEL_SEC_PULSE)
> +		return -EINVAL;

Should this return -EINVAL silently, or would -ENODATA/log a message be
more useful to callers (e.g. "date" command on an unprogrammed RTC)?
Not a blocker, just a readability/debuggability suggestion matching
what other U-Boot RTC drivers do when the clock hasn't been set yet.

=== Patch 2/3: riscv: sophgo: Enable RTC-backed system reset ===

> -	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_SYSRESET_CV1800B))
> -		device_bind_driver(gd->dm_root, "cv1800b_sysreset", "sysreset", NULL);
> -
>  	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NET_LEGACY))
>  		cv1800b_ephy_init();

Removing the board-level manual bind is correct now that patch 1 makes
the RTC driver bind the sysreset child itself. But this creates a
cross-patch ordering dependency: between patch 2 and patch 3 applied
alone (e.g. bisection), CONFIG_SYSRESET_CV1800B could still be set
without CONFIG_RTC_CV1800B generating the bind call, since patch 3 is
what adds "depends on RTC_CV1800B" to Kconfig. Worth double-checking
`git bisect`/incremental apply doesn't leave milkv_duo with sysreset
silently unbound between patch 2 and patch 3 landing -- it would be
safer if the Kconfig dependency change in patch 3 were folded into
patch 1, since patch 1 is what introduces the alternate bind mechanism
that patch 2 relies on removing the board_init() call.

> +CONFIG_DM_RTC=y
> +CONFIG_RTC_CV1800B=y

These three defconfigs add RTC_CV1800B but the diff doesn't show
CONFIG_SYSRESET_CV1800B being added/retained -- please confirm in the
full defconfig (not shown in this hunk) that SYSRESET_CV1800B is still
set for all three boards now that it depends on RTC_CV1800B, otherwise
power-off/reset would silently regress on affected boards.

=== Patch 3/3: sysreset: cv1800b: Use the RTC device node for register access ===

> +static int cv1800b_sysreset_request(struct udevice *dev, enum sysreset_t type)
> +{
> +	struct cv1800b_sysreset_priv *priv = dev_get_priv(dev);
> +	int ret;
> +
> +	if (type >= SYSRESET_COUNT)
> +		return -EINVAL;

Good addition -- the previous code indexed reset_info[] with an
unchecked `type`, so this closes a real out-of-bounds read for
sysreset types that aren't in the array (e.g. SYSRESET_POWER_ON).

> +	ret = regmap_write(priv->regmap, reset_info[type].pre_req_reg, 1);
> +	if (ret)
> +		return ret;
> +
> +	ret = regmap_write(priv->regmap, RTC_CTRL_UNLOCKKEY,
> +			   CTRL_UNLOCKKEY_MAGIC);
> +	if (ret)
> +		return ret;
> +
> +	ret = regmap_write(priv->regmap, RTC_CTRL,
> +			   0xffff0800 | reset_info[type].req_bit);
> +	if (ret)
> +		return ret;

Nice, this now propagates regmap errors on every step instead of the
previous fire-and-forget writel() calls -- matches the commit message's
"propagate register access errors" claim.

> -static const struct udevice_id cv1800b_sysreset_ids[] = {
> -	{ .compatible = "sophgo,cv1800b-sysreset", },
> -	{},
> -};
> -
>  U_BOOT_DRIVER(sysreset_cv1800b) = {
>  	.name = "cv1800b_sysreset",
> -	.id	  = UCLASS_SYSRESET,
> -	.ops  = &cv1800b_sysreset,
> -	.of_match = cv1800b_sysreset_ids
> +	.id = UCLASS_SYSRESET,
> +	.probe = cv1800b_sysreset_probe,
> +	.ops = &cv1800b_sysreset,
> +	.priv_auto = sizeof(struct cv1800b_sysreset_priv),
>  };

Removing of_match entirely means this driver can now *only* ever be
instantiated via device_bind_driver_to_node() from patch 1 -- it will
never match a devicetree node directly even if someone still has
"sophgo,cv1800b-sysreset" in an out-of-tree DTS. That's intentional
given the new design, but please mention this behavioral/ABI change
explicitly in the commit message (it's a removed compatible string,
which is the kind of thing that's easy to miss when reading just the
diffstat), and double check there's no in-tree devicetree still using
that compatible that would now silently fail to bind.

> config SYSRESET_CV1800B
> -	bool "Enable support for Sophgo cv1800b System Reset"
> +	bool "Enable support for Sophgo CV1800B system reset"
> +	depends on RTC_CV1800B

Since RTC_CV1800B already does `select REGMAP`, SYSRESET_CV1800B gets
REGMAP transitively through the "depends on" -- that's fine, but note
it only works because both options are reachable in the same Kconfig
pass; if SYSRESET_CV1800B ever gains an independent "select REGMAP" by
mistake in a future patch it'd be redundant but harmless. No action
needed here, just flagging the implicit dependency for anyone touching
this later.

=== Summary ===

- Please confirm the devicetree "reg" range for the shared RTC node
  covers all registers touched by both the RTC and sysreset drivers.
- Consider folding the Kconfig "depends on RTC_CV1800B" into patch 1
  (or reordering) to avoid an intermediate bisection state where
  SYSRESET_CV1800B can be enabled without the new bind mechanism.
- Confirm SYSRESET_CV1800B is still enabled in all three defconfigs
  touched by patch 2.
- Please call out the removed sysreset of_match/compatible string as
  a user-visible behavioral change in the patch 3 commit message.

Overall: needs a v3 (or author confirmation) addressing the above before merge.

Thanks,
Leo
